6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The Center for Applied Sciences

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Last time we began...

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Trying to...

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
We began by trying to navigate our way through...

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Kant's Moral Theory

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,500
Now, fully to make sense of Kant's moral theory in

12
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:54,000
the groundwork requires that we be able to answer three questions.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,500
How can duty and autonomy go together?

14
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,000
What's the great dignity in answering to duty?

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,500
It would seem that these two ideas are

16
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:12,000
opposed, duty and autonomy. What's Kant's answer to that?

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Need someone here to speak up on

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Kant's behalf. Does he have an answer?

19
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Yes, go ahead, stand up.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Kant believes that you only act autonomously when you

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
are pursuing something only in the name of duty

22
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
and not because of your own circumstances.

23
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Such as, like you're only doing something good

24
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
and moral if you're doing it because of duty

25
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
and not because of something of your own personal gain.

26
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Now why is that acting out, what's your name?

27
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Mine is Matt.

28
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,500
Matt, why is that acting out of

29
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:47,000
freedom? I hear what you're saying about duty.

30
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,500
Because you choose to accept those moral laws in yourself

31
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,000
and they're not brought on from outside upon onto you.

32
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,500
Okay, good. Because acting out of

33
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:59,000
duty is following a moral law...

34
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:00,000
That you impose on yourself.

35
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
That you impose on yourself. That's what makes...

36
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Yes.

37
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
...duty compatible with freedom.

38
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Yeah.

39
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Okay, that's good, Matt. That is

40
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Kant's answer. That's great, thank you.

41
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,500
So, Kant's answer is, it is not insofar as

42
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:19,000
I am subject to the law that I have dignity,

43
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,500
but rather insofar as with regard to

44
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:26,000
that very same law, I'm the author.

45
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
And I'm subordinated to that law on that

46
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
grounds that I took it, as Matt just said,

47
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
I took it upon myself. I willed that law.

48
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
So, that's why for Kant, acting according to

49
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
duty and acting freely, in the sense of autonomously,

50
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
are one and the same.

51
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
But that raises the question,

52
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
how many moral laws are there?

53
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,500
Because if dignity consists in being governed

54
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,000
by a law that I give myself,

55
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
what's to guarantee that my conscience,

56
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
my conscience will be the same as your conscience?

57
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Who has Kant's answer to that? Yes.

58
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,500
Because a moral law is

59
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,000
not contingent upon subjective conditions,

60
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
it would transcend all

61
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,000
particular differences between people,

62
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,000
and so would be a universal law.

63
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
And in this respect, there would only be

64
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
one moral law, because it would be supreme.

65
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Right. That's exactly right. What's your name?

66
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Kelly.

67
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Kelly.

68
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
So, Kelly, Kant believes

69
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
that if we choose freely, out

70
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
of our own consciences, the moral law,

71
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,500
we're guaranteed to come up with

72
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:40,000
one and the same moral law.

73
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Yes.

74
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
And that's because when I choose,

75
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
it's not me, Michael Sandel, choosing.

76
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
It's not you, Kelly, choosing for yourself.

77
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
What is it exactly?

78
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Who is doing the choosing?

79
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Who's the subject? Who's the agent who's doing the choosing?

80
00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Reason.

81
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Well, reason... Pure reason.

82
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Pure reason. And what you mean

83
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
by pure reason is what exactly?

84
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Well, pure reason is, like we were saying before,

85
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
not subject to any external conditions

86
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
that may be imposed on it.

87
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Good. That's great.

88
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,500
So, the reason that does the

89
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:21,000
willing, the reason that governs my will,

90
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
when I will the moral law,

91
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
is the same reason that operates when you choose,

92
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
the moral law, for yourself.

93
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Yes.

94
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
And that's why it's possible to

95
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
act autonomously, to choose for myself,

96
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,500
for each of us to

97
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:47,000
choose for ourselves, as autonomous beings,

98
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,000
and for all of us to

99
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
wind up willing the same moral law,

100
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
the categorical imperative.

101
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,500
But then there is one

102
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:57,000
big and very difficult question left.

103
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Even if you accept,

104
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
everything that Matt and Kelly have said so far.

105
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
How is a categorical imperative possible?

106
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
How is morality possible?

107
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
To answer that question, Kant says

108
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
we need to make a distinction.

109
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
We need to make a distinction between two standpoints.

110
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Two standpoints from which

111
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
we can make sense of our experience.

112
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Let me try to explain what

113
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
he means by these two standpoints.

114
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
As an object of experience,

115
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,000
I belong to the sensible world.

116
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
There, my actions are determined

117
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,000
by the laws of nature,

118
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
and by the regularities of cause and effect.

119
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
But as a subject of experience,

120
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
I inhabit an intelligible world,

121
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
and here, being independent of the laws of nature,

122
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
I am capable of autonomy,

123
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
capable of acting according to a law I give myself.

124
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Now Kant says that only from this second standpoint

125
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
can I regard myself as free,

126
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,500
for to be independent of determination

127
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:22,000
by causes in the sensible world

128
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
is to be free.

129
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
If I were wholly an empirical being,

130
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:34,000
as the utilitarians assume,

131
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:41,000
if I were a being wholly and only subject

132
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
to the deliverances of my senses,

133
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
to pain and pleasure and hunger and thirst and appetite,

134
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
if that's all there were to humanity,

135
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
we wouldn't be capable of freedom.

136
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Kant reasons.

137
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Because in that case,

138
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
every exercise of will

139
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,000
would be conditioned by the desire for some object.

140
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:11,000
In that case, all choice would be heteronymous choice,

141
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:16,000
governed by the pursuit of some external end.

142
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
When we think of ourselves as free, Kant writes,

143
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
we transfer ourselves

144
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
into the intelligible world as members

145
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:29,000
and recognize the autonomy of the will.

146
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
That's the idea of the two standpoints.

147
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:38,000
So, how are categorical imperatives possible?

148
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Only because the idea of freedom

149
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,000
makes me a member of an intelligible world.

150
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Now, Kant admits,

151
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,000
we aren't only rational beings,

152
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
we don't only, in a sense,

153
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
only inhabit the intelligible world,

154
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
the realm of freedom.

155
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:02,000
If we did,

156
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
if we did,

157
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
then all of our actions would invariably accord

158
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:15,000
with the autonomy of the will.

159
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:19,000
But precisely because we inhabit simultaneously

160
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
the two standpoints, the two realms,

161
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,000
the realm of freedom

162
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:30,000
and the realm of necessity,

163
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
precisely because we inhabit both realms,

164
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:37,000
there is always potentially a gap

165
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
between what we do

166
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
and what we ought to do,

167
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
between is and ought.

168
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
Another way of putting this point,

169
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
and this is the point

170
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
with which Kant concludes the groundwork,

171
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
morality is not

172
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:54,000
not empirical.

173
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
Whatever you see in the world,

174
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
whatever you discover through science

175
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:05,000
can't decide moral questions.

176
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:10,000
Morality stands at a certain distance from the world,

177
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
from the empirical world.

178
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:18,000
And that's why no science could deliver moral truth.

179
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Now, I want to test Kant's moral theory

180
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
with the hardest possible case,

181
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
a case that he raises,

182
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:33,000
the case of the murderer at the door.

183
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
Kant says that lying is wrong.

184
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:36,000
We all know that.

185
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,000
We've discussed why.

186
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:45,000
Lying is at odds with the categorical imperative.

187
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:49,000
A French philosopher, Benjamin Constant,

188
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
wrote an article,

189
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
responding to the groundwork,

190
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
where he said,

191
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
this absolute prohibition on lying is wrong.

192
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
It can't be right.

193
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:05,000
What if a murderer came to your door

194
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
looking for your friend who was hiding in your house,

195
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:14,000
and the murderer asked you, point blank,

196
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
is your friend in your house?

197
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Constant says it would be crazy

198
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:21,500
to say that the moral

199
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:23,000
thing to do in that case

200
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,000
is to tell the truth.

201
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Constant says the murderer

202
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,000
certainly doesn't deserve the truth.

203
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
And Kant wrote a reply.

204
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:42,000
And Kant stuck by his principle

205
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,000
that lying even to the murderer at the door

206
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
is wrong.

207
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,000
And the reason it's wrong, he said,

208
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:58,000
is once you start taking consequences into account

209
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
to carve out exceptions to the categorical imperative,

210
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
you've given up the whole moral framework.

211
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,000
You've become a consequentialist,

212
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
or maybe a rule utilitarian.

213
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
But most of you,

214
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
and most of Kant's readers,

215
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
think there's something odd

216
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:22,000
or something plausible about this answer.

217
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
I would like to try to defend Kant

218
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
on this point.

219
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
And then I want to see whether you

220
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
think that my defense is plausible.

221
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
And I would want to defend him

222
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:41,000
within the spirit of his own account of morality.

223
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
Imagine that someone comes to your door.

224
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
You were asked the question by this murderer.

225
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
You're hiding your friend.

226
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
Is there a way

227
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:54,000
that you could avoid telling a lie

228
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:00,000
without selling out your friend?

229
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Does anyone have an idea

230
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
of how you might be able to do that?

231
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Yes.

232
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Stand up.

233
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,000
I was just going to say

234
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
if I were to let my friend

235
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
in my house to hide in the first place,

236
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
I'd probably make a plan with them.

237
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,000
So I'd be like,

238
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
hey, I'll tell the murderer you're here.

239
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
But escape.

240
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:23,000
And that's one of the options mentioned.

241
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
But I'm not sure that's a Kantian option.

242
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
You're still lying, though.

243
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
No, because he's in the house,

244
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:31,000
but he won't be.

245
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Oh, I see.

246
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:37,000
All right, good enough.

247
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:38,000
One more try.

248
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
If you just say you don't know where he is

249
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
because he might not be locked in the closet,

250
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
he might have left the closet,

251
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,000
you have no clue where he could be.

252
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:50,000
So you would say, I don't know,

253
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
which wouldn't actually be a lie

254
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:57,000
because you weren't at that very moment

255
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:58,000
looking in the closet.

256
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
Exactly.

257
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:04,000
So it would be, strictly speaking, true.

258
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,000
Yes.

259
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:10,000
And yet possibly deceiving.

260
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Misleading.

261
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,000
But still true.

262
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,000
What's your name?

263
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,000
I'm David.

264
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,000
I'm a lawyer.

265
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,000
What's your name?

266
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,000
John.

267
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:19,000
John.

268
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:20,000
All right, John has...

269
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Now, John may be on to something.

270
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
John, you're really offering us

271
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
the option of a clever evasion

272
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
that is, strictly speaking, true.

273
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
This raises the question

274
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
whether there is a moral difference

275
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:41,000
between an outright lie

276
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,000
and a misleading truth.

277
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
From Kant's point of view,

278
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:51,000
there actually is a world of difference

279
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:58,000
between a lie and a misleading truth.

280
00:13:58,000 --> 00:13:59,000
Why is that?

281
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Even though both might have the same consequences.

282
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,000
But then remember,

283
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Kant doesn't base morality on consequences.

284
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:14,000
He bases it on formal adherence to the moral law.

285
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,000
Now, sometimes,

286
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
in ordinary life,

287
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,000
we make exceptions

288
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
for the general rule against lying

289
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
with a white lie.

290
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
What is a white lie?

291
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:27,000
It's a lie to make...

292
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
Well, to avoid hurting someone's feelings,

293
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
for example.

294
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,000
It's a lie that we think of

295
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
as justified by the consequences.

296
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Now, Kant could not endorse a white lie,

297
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
but perhaps he could endorse

298
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
a misleading truth.

299
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
Suppose someone gives you a tie.

300
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
It's a gift.

301
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
And you open the box,

302
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
and it's just awful.

303
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
What do you say?

304
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:54,000
Thank you.

305
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:55,000
Thank you?

306
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
You could say thank you.

307
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
But they're waiting to see what you think of it.

308
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:01,000
Or they ask you,

309
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:02,000
what do you think of it?

310
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
You could tell a white lie and say,

311
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
it's beautiful.

312
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
But that wouldn't be permissible

313
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
from Kant's point of view.

314
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
Could you say,

315
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
not a white lie, but a misleading truth?

316
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
You open the box and you say,

317
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
I've never seen a tie like that before.

318
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
Thank you.

319
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,000
You shouldn't have.

320
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
You shouldn't have.

321
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
That's good.

322
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:41,000
Can you think of a contemporary political leader

323
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
who engaged...

324
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
You can?

325
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
Who are you thinking of?

326
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:54,000
Remember the whole carefully worded denials

327
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:58,000
in the Monica Lewinsky affair of Bill Clinton?

328
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:03,000
Now, those denials actually became

329
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:08,000
the subject of very explicit debate and argument

330
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
during the impeachment hearings.

331
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:15,000
Take a look at the following

332
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
excerpts from Bill Clinton.

333
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
Is there something, do you think,

334
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:22,000
morally at stake in the distinction

335
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:25,000
between a lie

336
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:30,000
and a misleading, carefully couched truth?

337
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
I want to say one thing to the American people.

338
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
I want you to listen to me.

339
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
I'm going to say this again.

340
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:44,000
I did not have sexual relations with that woman,

341
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Ms. Lewinsky.

342
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
I never told anybody to lie,

343
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
not a single time, never.

344
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
These allegations are false.

345
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
Did he lie to the American people

346
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
when he said, I never had sex with that woman?

347
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
You know, he doesn't believe he did,

348
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:05,000
and because of the...

349
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
Let me explain, Congressman.

350
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
What he said was to the American people

351
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
that he did not have sexual relations.

352
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
And I understand you're not going to like this,

353
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
Congressman, because you will see it

354
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,000
as a hair-splitting, evasive answer.

355
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:24,000
But in his own mind, his definition was not...

356
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Okay, I understand that argument.

357
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Okay.

358
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:29,000
All right.

359
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
So there you have the exchange.

360
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Now, at the time, you may have thought

361
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,000
this was just a legalistic, hair-splitting exchange

362
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,000
between a Republican who wanted to impeach Clinton

363
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,000
and a lawyer who was trying to defend him.

364
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:48,000
But now, in the light of Kant,

365
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:53,000
do you think there is something morally at stake

366
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:57,000
in the distinction between a lie

367
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and an evasion,

368
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:04,000
a true but misleading statement?

369
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:08,000
I'd like to hear from defenders of Kant,

370
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
people who think there is a distinction.

371
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
Are you ready to defend Kant?

372
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
Well, I think when you try to say that lying

373
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
and misleading truths are the same thing,

374
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
you're basing it on a consequentialist argument,

375
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
which is that they achieve the same thing.

376
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:22,500
But the fact of the

377
00:18:22,500 --> 00:18:24,000
matter is, you told the truth,

378
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
and you intended that people would believe

379
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
what you were saying, which was the truth,

380
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:29,000
which means it is not morally

381
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:30,000
the same as telling a lie

382
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
and intending that they believe it is the truth,

383
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
even though it's not true.

384
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,000
Good. What's your name?

385
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
Diana.

386
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
So Diana says that Kant has a point here.

387
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,500
And it's a point that might even

388
00:18:42,500 --> 00:18:45,000
come to the aid of Bill Clinton.

389
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
And that is...

390
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
Well, what about that?

391
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
Someone over here.

392
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
For Kant, motivation is key.

393
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:57,000
So if you give to someone because primarily

394
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:58,000
you want to feel good about yourself,

395
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Kant would say that has no moral worth.

396
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
Well, with this, the motivation is the same.

397
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
It's to sort of mislead someone, it's to lie,

398
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
it's to sort of throw them off the track,

399
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:07,000
and the motivation is the same.

400
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
So there should be no difference.

401
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:10,000
Okay, good.

402
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:13,000
So here, isn't the motive the same, Diana?

403
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
What do you say to this argument that,

404
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
well, the motive is the same.

405
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
In both cases, there is the attempt,

406
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:21,000
or at least the hope,

407
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,000
that one's pursuer will be misled.

408
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
Well, that...

409
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
You could look at it that way,

410
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
but I think that the fact is that

411
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
your immediate motive is that they should believe you.

412
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:33,500
The ultimate consequence of that

413
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:35,000
is that they might be deceived

414
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
and not find out what was going on.

415
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
But your immediate motive is that they should believe you

416
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
because you're telling the truth.

417
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:42,000
May I help a little?

418
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:43,000
Sure.

419
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
You and Kant.

420
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:46,000
Why don't you say...

421
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
And what's your name? I'm sorry.

422
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:49,000
Wes.

423
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:52,000
Why don't you say to Wesley,

424
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:55,000
it's not exactly the case

425
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:58,000
that the motive in both cases

426
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
is to mislead.

427
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:04,000
They're hoping, they're hoping that the person

428
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:08,000
will be misled by the statement,

429
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
I don't know where they are,

430
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:14,000
or I never had sexual relations.

431
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
You're hoping that they will be misled,

432
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:20,000
but in the case where you're telling the truth,

433
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:23,000
your motive is to mislead while at the same time

434
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:26,000
telling the truth and honoring the moral law

435
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,000
and staying within the bounds of the categorical imperative.

436
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
I think Kant's answer would be...

437
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
Diana, yes?

438
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:34,000
Yes.

439
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:35,000
You like that?

440
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:36,000
I do.

441
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:37,000
Okay.

442
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:38,000
So my answer would be,

443
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:42,000
unlike a falsehood, unlike a lie,

444
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:48,000
a misleading truth pays a certain homage to duty.

445
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
And the homage it pays to duty

446
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
is what justifies that the work,

447
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:55,000
even the work of evasion.

448
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
Diana, yes?

449
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:58,000
You like...

450
00:20:58,000 --> 00:20:59,000
Okay.

451
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:03,000
And so there is something,

452
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:06,000
some element of respect

453
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:10,000
for the dignity of the moral law

454
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:13,000
in the careful evasion,

455
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:17,000
because Clinton could have told an outright lie,

456
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
but he didn't.

457
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,000
And so I think Kant's...

458
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
Kant's insight here is,

459
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:29,000
in the carefully couched but true evasion,

460
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:30,500
there is a kind of homage

461
00:21:30,500 --> 00:21:32,000
to the dignity of the moral law

462
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:35,000
that is not present in the outright lie.

463
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:38,000
And that, Wesley,

464
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
is part of the motive.

465
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:41,000
It's part of the motive.

466
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
Yes, I hope he will be misled.

467
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:46,000
I hope the murderer will run down the road

468
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
or go to the mall looking for my friend

469
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:49,000
instead of the closet.

470
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
I hope that will be the effect.

471
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
I can't control that.

472
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
I can't control the consequences.

473
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
But what I can control

474
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:01,000
is standing by and honoring,

475
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:02,000
however I pursue,

476
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
the ends I hope will unfold.

477
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
To do so in a way

478
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:10,000
that is consistent with respect for the moral law.

479
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:13,000
Wesley, I don't think, is entirely persuaded.

480
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
But at least this brings out,

481
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
this discussion brings out,

482
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:18,000
some of what's at stake,

483
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
what's morally at stake

484
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:23,000
in Kant's notion of the categorical imperative.

485
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
As long as any effort is involved

486
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:32,000
in the moral law,

487
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:33,000
it's not a matter of

488
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
whether the effort is involved.

489
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:38,000
I would say that the contract is valid

490
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:41,000
and it should take effect.

491
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:42,000
But why?

492
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,000
What was, what morally can you point to?

493
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
For example, two people agree to be married

494
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
and one suddenly calls the other

495
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:50,000
and in two minutes say,

496
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:51,000
I changed my mind.

497
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:55,000
Does the contract have obligation on both sides?

498
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
Well, I'm tempted to say no.

499
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:02,000
Fine.

500
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:04,000
Thank you.

501
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:05,480
Thank you,

502
00:23:05,480 --> 00:23:07,000
thank you.

503
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:08,000
Thank you.

504
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:09,000
Last time,

505
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:12,000
we talked about Kant's categorical imperative

506
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:15,000
and we considered the way he applied

507
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:18,000
the idea of the categorical imperative

508
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:22,200
to the case of lying.

509
00:23:22,200 --> 00:23:25,000
I want to turn briefly to one other application

510
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
of Kant's moral theory

511
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:30,000
and that's his political theory.

512
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
Now, Kant says,

513
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:36,120
that just laws arise from

514
00:23:36,120 --> 00:23:39,240
a certain kind of social contract.

515
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:42,240
But this contract, he tells

516
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:45,240
us, is of an exceptional nature.

517
00:23:45,240 --> 00:23:49,740
What makes the contract exceptional is

518
00:23:49,740 --> 00:23:54,240
that it's not an actual contract

519
00:23:54,240 --> 00:23:58,150
that happens when people come together and try

520
00:23:58,150 --> 00:24:02,060
to figure out what the Constitution should be.

521
00:24:02,060 --> 00:24:08,530
Kant points out that the contract that generates

522
00:24:08,530 --> 00:24:15,000
justice is what he calls an idea of reason.

523
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:18,130
It's not an actual contract among actual

524
00:24:18,130 --> 00:24:21,260
men and women gathered in a constitutional convention.

525
00:24:21,260 --> 00:24:24,159
Why not?

526
00:24:24,159 --> 00:24:27,229
I think Kant's reason is that actual men

527
00:24:27,229 --> 00:24:30,300
and women gathered in a real constitutional convention

528
00:24:30,300 --> 00:24:32,000
would have different interests,

529
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:32,020
would have different interests,

530
00:24:32,020 --> 00:24:32,040
would have different interests,

531
00:24:32,040 --> 00:24:33,080
values, aims,

532
00:24:33,560 --> 00:24:36,100
and there would also be differences of bargaining power

533
00:24:36,100 --> 00:24:38,440
and differences of knowledge among them.

534
00:24:39,940 --> 00:24:43,060
And so the laws that would result from their deliberations

535
00:24:43,060 --> 00:24:45,980
wouldn't necessarily be just,

536
00:24:46,620 --> 00:24:49,620
wouldn't necessarily conform to principles of right,

537
00:24:50,520 --> 00:24:52,480
but would simply reflect

538
00:24:52,480 --> 00:24:54,440
the differences of bargaining power,

539
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:56,020
the special interests,

540
00:24:56,399 --> 00:24:58,480
the fact that some might know more than others

541
00:24:58,480 --> 00:25:02,000
about law or about politics.

542
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:09,000
So Kant says a contract that generates principles of right

543
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:14,440
is merely an idea of reason,

544
00:25:14,440 --> 00:25:17,660
but it has undoubted practical reality,

545
00:25:17,660 --> 00:25:20,110
because it can oblige every legislator to

546
00:25:20,110 --> 00:25:22,560
frame his laws in such a way

547
00:25:22,560 --> 00:25:24,740
that they could have been produced by

548
00:25:24,740 --> 00:25:26,920
the united will of the whole nation.

549
00:25:26,920 --> 00:25:30,460
So Kant is a contractarian,

550
00:25:30,460 --> 00:25:31,040
but he does not believe that the laws that

551
00:25:31,040 --> 00:25:31,620
he has created, the laws that he has created,

552
00:25:31,620 --> 00:25:31,690
are the laws that he has created. He

553
00:25:31,690 --> 00:25:31,760
believes that the laws that he has created,

554
00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:32,260
are the laws that he has created,

555
00:25:32,260 --> 00:25:32,760
he believes that the laws that he has created,

556
00:25:32,760 --> 00:25:33,260
he believes that the laws that he has created,

557
00:25:33,260 --> 00:25:33,760
are the laws that he has created,

558
00:25:33,760 --> 00:25:35,510
and he doesn't trace the

559
00:25:35,510 --> 00:25:37,260
origin or the rightness of law

560
00:25:37,260 --> 00:25:40,260
to any actual social contract.

561
00:25:41,640 --> 00:25:44,340
This gives rise to an obvious question.

562
00:25:44,340 --> 00:25:46,340
What is the moral force

563
00:25:49,300 --> 00:25:51,280
of a hypothetical contract,

564
00:25:51,280 --> 00:25:53,280
a contract that never happened?

565
00:25:54,700 --> 00:25:57,080
That's the question we take up today.

566
00:25:57,080 --> 00:25:58,620
But in order to investigate it,

567
00:25:58,620 --> 00:26:01,720
we need to turn to a modern philosophy,

568
00:26:01,720 --> 00:26:03,720
John Rawls,

569
00:26:03,720 --> 00:26:05,720
who worked out in his book,

570
00:26:05,720 --> 00:26:07,720
A Theory of Justice,

571
00:26:07,720 --> 00:26:09,720
in great detail,

572
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:12,720
an account of a hypothetical agreement

573
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:14,720
as the basis for justice.

574
00:26:14,720 --> 00:26:17,720
Rawls' theory of justice,

575
00:26:17,720 --> 00:26:20,720
in broad outline,

576
00:26:20,720 --> 00:26:24,720
is parallel to Kant's in two important respects.

577
00:26:24,720 --> 00:26:26,720
Like Kant,

578
00:26:26,720 --> 00:26:30,720
Rawls was a critic of utilitarianism.

579
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:31,600
Rawls was a critic of utilitarianism.

580
00:26:31,600 --> 00:26:33,600
Rawls was a critic of utilitarianism.

581
00:26:33,600 --> 00:26:36,600
Each person possesses an inviolability

582
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:38,600
founded on justice, Rawls writes,

583
00:26:38,600 --> 00:26:40,600
that even the welfare of

584
00:26:40,600 --> 00:26:42,600
society as a whole cannot override.

585
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:44,600
The rights secured by justice

586
00:26:44,600 --> 00:26:47,600
are not subject to political bargaining

587
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:50,600
or to the calculus of social interests.

588
00:26:50,600 --> 00:26:53,600
The second respect

589
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:56,600
in which Rawls' theory

590
00:26:56,600 --> 00:27:00,600
follows Kant's

591
00:27:00,600 --> 00:27:02,600
is on the idea

592
00:27:02,600 --> 00:27:06,600
that principles of justice,

593
00:27:06,600 --> 00:27:08,600
properly understood,

594
00:27:08,600 --> 00:27:13,600
can be derived from a hypothetical social contract,

595
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:16,600
not an actual one.

596
00:27:16,600 --> 00:27:18,600
And Rawls works this out

597
00:27:18,600 --> 00:27:21,600
in fascinating detail

598
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:23,600
with the device

599
00:27:23,600 --> 00:27:28,600
of what he calls the veil of ignorance.

600
00:27:28,600 --> 00:27:30,600
The way to arrive at a rule of law,

601
00:27:30,600 --> 00:27:32,600
is to arrive

602
00:27:32,600 --> 00:27:34,600
at the rights,

603
00:27:34,600 --> 00:27:36,600
the basic rights

604
00:27:36,600 --> 00:27:39,600
that we must respect.

605
00:27:39,600 --> 00:27:42,600
The basic framework of rights and duties

606
00:27:42,600 --> 00:27:45,600
is to imagine

607
00:27:45,600 --> 00:27:48,600
that we were gathered together

608
00:27:48,600 --> 00:27:50,600
trying to choose the principles

609
00:27:50,600 --> 00:27:54,600
to govern our collective lives

610
00:27:54,600 --> 00:27:56,600
without knowing

611
00:27:56,600 --> 00:27:59,600
certain important particular facts

612
00:27:59,600 --> 00:28:02,600
about ourselves.

613
00:28:02,600 --> 00:28:05,600
That's the idea of the veil of ignorance.

614
00:28:05,600 --> 00:28:07,600
Now what would happen if we

615
00:28:07,600 --> 00:28:09,600
gathered together,

616
00:28:09,600 --> 00:28:11,600
just as we are here,

617
00:28:11,600 --> 00:28:13,600
and tried to come up with principles of justice

618
00:28:13,600 --> 00:28:16,600
to govern our collective life?

619
00:28:16,600 --> 00:28:18,600
There would be a cacophony of proposals,

620
00:28:18,600 --> 00:28:20,600
of suggestions,

621
00:28:20,600 --> 00:28:23,600
reflecting people's different interests.

622
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:25,600
Some are strong, some are weak.

623
00:28:25,600 --> 00:28:28,600
Some are rich, some are poor.

624
00:28:28,600 --> 00:28:30,600
So Rawls says,

625
00:28:30,600 --> 00:28:32,600
imagine instead

626
00:28:32,600 --> 00:28:33,600
that we are gathered

627
00:28:33,600 --> 00:28:38,600
in an original position of equality.

628
00:28:38,600 --> 00:28:42,600
And what assures the equality

629
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:45,600
is the veil of ignorance.

630
00:28:45,600 --> 00:28:47,600
Imagine that we are all behind

631
00:28:47,600 --> 00:28:48,600
a veil of ignorance

632
00:28:48,600 --> 00:28:51,600
which temporarily

633
00:28:51,600 --> 00:28:54,600
abstracts from, or brackets,

634
00:28:54,600 --> 00:28:57,600
hides from us.

635
00:28:58,600 --> 00:29:02,600
Who in particular we are.

636
00:29:02,600 --> 00:29:05,600
Our race, our class, our place in society,

637
00:29:05,600 --> 00:29:08,600
our strengths, our weaknesses,

638
00:29:08,600 --> 00:29:10,600
whether we're healthy or unhealthy.

639
00:29:10,600 --> 00:29:13,600
Then, and only then,

640
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:14,600
Rawls says,

641
00:29:14,600 --> 00:29:16,600
the principles we would agree to

642
00:29:16,600 --> 00:29:19,600
would be principles of justice.

643
00:29:19,600 --> 00:29:24,600
That's how the hypothetical contract works.

644
00:29:24,600 --> 00:29:27,600
What is the moral force

645
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:32,600
of this kind of hypothetical agreement?

646
00:29:32,600 --> 00:29:35,600
Is it stronger or weaker than a real agreement,

647
00:29:35,600 --> 00:29:38,600
an actual social contract?

648
00:29:43,600 --> 00:29:46,600
In order to answer that question,

649
00:29:46,600 --> 00:29:50,600
we have to look hard at the moral force

650
00:29:50,600 --> 00:29:53,600
of actual contracts.

651
00:29:53,600 --> 00:29:56,600
There are really two questions here.

652
00:29:57,600 --> 00:29:59,600
One of them is,

653
00:29:59,600 --> 00:30:02,600
how do actual contracts bind me,

654
00:30:02,600 --> 00:30:04,600
or obligate me?

655
00:30:04,600 --> 00:30:06,600
Question number one.

656
00:30:06,600 --> 00:30:08,600
And question number two,

657
00:30:08,600 --> 00:30:11,600
how do actual, real life contracts

658
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:14,600
justify the terms that they produce?

659
00:30:14,600 --> 00:30:16,600
If you think about it,

660
00:30:16,600 --> 00:30:20,600
this is in line with Rawls and Kant,

661
00:30:20,600 --> 00:30:23,600
the answer to the second question,

662
00:30:23,600 --> 00:30:25,600
how do actual contracts justify

663
00:30:25,600 --> 00:30:26,600
the terms that they produce,

664
00:30:26,600 --> 00:30:30,600
the answer is they don't.

665
00:30:30,600 --> 00:30:32,600
At least not on their own.

666
00:30:32,600 --> 00:30:34,600
Actual contracts are not

667
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:38,600
self-sufficient moral instruments.

668
00:30:38,600 --> 00:30:42,600
Of any actual contract or agreement,

669
00:30:42,600 --> 00:30:45,600
it can always be asked,

670
00:30:45,600 --> 00:30:50,600
is it fair what they agreed to?

671
00:30:50,600 --> 00:30:52,600
The fact of the agreement

672
00:30:52,600 --> 00:30:54,600
never guarantees the fairness of the agreement.

673
00:30:54,600 --> 00:30:56,600
And we know this by

674
00:30:56,600 --> 00:30:59,600
looking at our own constitutional convention.

675
00:30:59,600 --> 00:31:03,600
It produced a constitution

676
00:31:03,600 --> 00:31:06,600
that permitted slavery to persist.

677
00:31:06,600 --> 00:31:08,600
It was agreed to,

678
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:10,600
it was an actual contract,

679
00:31:10,600 --> 00:31:13,600
but that doesn't establish

680
00:31:13,600 --> 00:31:15,600
that the laws agreed to,

681
00:31:15,600 --> 00:31:17,600
all of them, were just.

682
00:31:20,600 --> 00:31:22,600
Well then, what is the moral force

683
00:31:22,600 --> 00:31:24,600
of actual contracts?

684
00:31:24,600 --> 00:31:27,600
To the extent that they bind us,

685
00:31:27,600 --> 00:31:30,600
they obligate in two ways.

686
00:31:30,600 --> 00:31:32,600
Suppose, maybe here it would help

687
00:31:32,600 --> 00:31:35,600
to take an example.

688
00:31:35,600 --> 00:31:38,600
We make an agreement,

689
00:31:38,600 --> 00:31:42,600
a commercial agreement.

690
00:31:42,600 --> 00:31:45,600
I promise to pay you $100

691
00:31:45,600 --> 00:31:49,600
if you will go harvest

692
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:52,600
and bring to me 100 lobsters.

693
00:31:52,600 --> 00:31:55,600
We make a deal.

694
00:31:55,600 --> 00:31:57,600
You go out and harvest them

695
00:31:57,600 --> 00:31:59,600
and bring them to me.

696
00:31:59,600 --> 00:32:00,600
I eat the lobsters,

697
00:32:00,600 --> 00:32:02,600
serve them to my friends,

698
00:32:02,600 --> 00:32:04,600
and then I don't pay.

699
00:32:04,600 --> 00:32:05,600
And you say,

700
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:07,600
but you're obligated.

701
00:32:07,600 --> 00:32:09,600
And I say, why?

702
00:32:09,600 --> 00:32:10,600
What do you say?

703
00:32:10,600 --> 00:32:12,600
Well, we had a deal.

704
00:32:16,600 --> 00:32:19,600
And you benefited.

705
00:32:19,600 --> 00:32:21,600
You ate all those lobsters.

706
00:32:22,600 --> 00:32:27,600
Well, that's a pretty strong argument.

707
00:32:27,600 --> 00:32:30,600
It's an argument that depends, though,

708
00:32:30,600 --> 00:32:33,600
on the fact that I benefited

709
00:32:33,600 --> 00:32:36,600
from your labor.

710
00:32:36,600 --> 00:32:40,600
So, contracts sometimes bind us

711
00:32:40,600 --> 00:32:43,600
insofar as they are instruments

712
00:32:43,600 --> 00:32:46,600
of mutual benefit.

713
00:32:46,600 --> 00:32:47,600
I ate the lobsters.

714
00:32:47,600 --> 00:32:49,600
I owe you the $100

715
00:32:49,600 --> 00:32:51,600
for having gathered them.

716
00:32:52,600 --> 00:32:53,600
But suppose,

717
00:32:53,600 --> 00:32:54,600
now take a second case,

718
00:32:54,600 --> 00:32:56,600
we make this deal.

719
00:32:56,600 --> 00:32:58,600
I'll pay you $100

720
00:32:58,600 --> 00:32:59,600
for 100 lobsters.

721
00:32:59,600 --> 00:33:01,600
And two minutes later,

722
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:03,600
before you've gone to any work,

723
00:33:03,600 --> 00:33:04,600
I call you back and say,

724
00:33:04,600 --> 00:33:07,600
I've changed my mind.

725
00:33:07,600 --> 00:33:09,600
Now,

726
00:33:09,600 --> 00:33:13,600
there's no benefit.

727
00:33:13,600 --> 00:33:16,600
There's no work on your part.

728
00:33:16,600 --> 00:33:17,600
So there's no element

729
00:33:17,600 --> 00:33:20,600
of reciprocal exchange.

730
00:33:20,600 --> 00:33:21,600
What about in that case?

731
00:33:21,600 --> 00:33:23,600
Do I still owe you?

732
00:33:23,600 --> 00:33:25,600
Merely in virtue of the fact

733
00:33:25,600 --> 00:33:27,600
that we had an agreement?

734
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:28,600
Who says,

735
00:33:28,600 --> 00:33:29,600
those of you who say yes,

736
00:33:29,600 --> 00:33:31,600
I still owe you?

737
00:33:31,600 --> 00:33:33,600
Why?

738
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:34,600
Okay, stand up.

739
00:33:34,600 --> 00:33:35,600
Why do I owe you?

740
00:33:35,600 --> 00:33:37,600
I call you back.

741
00:33:37,600 --> 00:33:38,600
After two minutes,

742
00:33:38,600 --> 00:33:39,600
you haven't done any work.

743
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:41,600
I think I spent the time

744
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:43,600
and effort in drafting

745
00:33:43,600 --> 00:33:45,600
this contract with you.

746
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:47,600
And also, I have emotional expectation

747
00:33:47,600 --> 00:33:49,600
that I'll go through the work.

748
00:33:49,600 --> 00:33:50,600
So you took time

749
00:33:50,600 --> 00:33:51,600
to draft the contract,

750
00:33:51,600 --> 00:33:52,600
but we did it very quickly.

751
00:33:52,600 --> 00:33:55,600
We just chatted on the phone.

752
00:33:55,600 --> 00:33:57,600
That wouldn't be a formal form

753
00:33:57,600 --> 00:33:58,600
of contract, though.

754
00:33:58,600 --> 00:33:59,600
Well, I faxed it to you.

755
00:33:59,600 --> 00:34:02,600
It only took a minute.

756
00:34:02,600 --> 00:34:05,600
As long as any effort is involved,

757
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:07,600
I would say that the contract

758
00:34:07,600 --> 00:34:11,600
is valid and it should take effect.

759
00:34:11,600 --> 00:34:12,600
But why?

760
00:34:12,600 --> 00:34:13,600
What was, what morally

761
00:34:13,600 --> 00:34:15,600
can you point to

762
00:34:15,600 --> 00:34:18,600
that obligates me?

763
00:34:18,600 --> 00:34:20,600
I admit that I agreed.

764
00:34:20,600 --> 00:34:23,600
But you didn't go to any work.

765
00:34:23,600 --> 00:34:25,600
I didn't enjoy any benefit.

766
00:34:25,600 --> 00:34:27,600
Because we might mentally

767
00:34:27,600 --> 00:34:28,600
go through all the work

768
00:34:28,600 --> 00:34:30,600
of harvesting the lobsters.

769
00:34:30,600 --> 00:34:31,600
You mentally went through

770
00:34:31,600 --> 00:34:34,600
the work of harvesting the lobsters.

771
00:34:34,600 --> 00:34:37,600
That's nothing, is it?

772
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:38,600
It's not much.

773
00:34:38,600 --> 00:34:39,600
Is it worth $100

774
00:34:39,600 --> 00:34:41,600
that you were imagining yourself

775
00:34:41,600 --> 00:34:43,600
going and collecting that?

776
00:34:43,600 --> 00:34:45,600
It may not worth $100,

777
00:34:45,600 --> 00:34:46,600
but it may worth something

778
00:34:46,600 --> 00:34:47,600
to some people.

779
00:34:47,600 --> 00:34:48,600
All right, I'll give you

780
00:34:48,600 --> 00:34:50,600
a buck for it, for that.

781
00:34:50,600 --> 00:34:52,600
But what I, so you're still pointing,

782
00:34:52,600 --> 00:34:53,600
what's interesting,

783
00:34:53,600 --> 00:34:55,600
you're still pointing to

784
00:34:55,600 --> 00:34:57,600
the reciprocal dimension of contracts.

785
00:34:57,600 --> 00:34:59,600
You did or imagined that you did

786
00:34:59,600 --> 00:35:01,600
or looked forward to doing something

787
00:35:01,600 --> 00:35:02,600
on my behalf.

788
00:35:02,600 --> 00:35:03,600
For example, two people agree

789
00:35:03,600 --> 00:35:04,600
to be married,

790
00:35:04,600 --> 00:35:05,600
and one suddenly calls the other

791
00:35:05,600 --> 00:35:06,600
and in two minutes say,

792
00:35:06,600 --> 00:35:07,600
I changed my mind.

793
00:35:07,600 --> 00:35:09,600
Does the contract have obligation

794
00:35:09,600 --> 00:35:12,600
on both sides?

795
00:35:14,600 --> 00:35:16,600
Nobody has paid any work

796
00:35:16,600 --> 00:35:18,600
or nobody has benefited yet.

797
00:35:18,600 --> 00:35:19,600
So, I'm not sure

798
00:35:19,600 --> 00:35:21,600
if I've benefited yet.

799
00:35:23,600 --> 00:35:25,600
Well, I'm tempted to say no.

800
00:35:28,600 --> 00:35:30,600
Fine.

801
00:35:34,600 --> 00:35:35,600
All right.

802
00:35:35,600 --> 00:35:36,600
What's your name?

803
00:35:36,600 --> 00:35:37,600
Julian.

804
00:35:37,600 --> 00:35:38,600
Thank you, Julian.

805
00:35:38,600 --> 00:35:39,600
All right.

806
00:35:39,600 --> 00:35:41,600
That was good.

807
00:35:41,600 --> 00:35:44,600
Now, is there anyone who has,

808
00:35:44,600 --> 00:35:46,600
who agrees with Julian

809
00:35:46,600 --> 00:35:48,600
that I still owe the money?

810
00:35:49,600 --> 00:35:51,600
For any other reason now,

811
00:35:51,600 --> 00:35:53,600
I have, go ahead.

812
00:35:53,600 --> 00:35:54,600
Stand up.

813
00:35:54,600 --> 00:35:55,600
I think if you back out,

814
00:35:55,600 --> 00:35:56,600
it sort of cheapens

815
00:35:56,600 --> 00:35:58,600
the institution of contracts.

816
00:35:58,600 --> 00:35:59,600
Good, but why?

817
00:35:59,600 --> 00:36:00,600
Why does it?

818
00:36:00,600 --> 00:36:02,600
Well, I think this is kind of Kantian,

819
00:36:02,600 --> 00:36:04,600
but there's, you know, almost,

820
00:36:04,600 --> 00:36:06,600
there's a certain intrinsic value

821
00:36:06,600 --> 00:36:07,600
in being able to make contracts

822
00:36:07,600 --> 00:36:08,600
and having, you know,

823
00:36:08,600 --> 00:36:10,600
knowing that people will expect

824
00:36:10,600 --> 00:36:12,600
that you'll go through with that.

825
00:36:12,600 --> 00:36:13,600
Good.

826
00:36:13,600 --> 00:36:14,600
There is some,

827
00:36:14,600 --> 00:36:16,600
it would cheapen the whole idea

828
00:36:16,600 --> 00:36:17,600
of contracts,

829
00:36:17,600 --> 00:36:18,600
which has to do with taking in,

830
00:36:18,600 --> 00:36:21,600
obligation on myself.

831
00:36:21,600 --> 00:36:23,600
Is that, is that the idea?

832
00:36:23,600 --> 00:36:24,600
Yeah, I think so.

833
00:36:24,600 --> 00:36:25,600
What's your name?

834
00:36:25,600 --> 00:36:26,600
Adam.

835
00:36:26,600 --> 00:36:28,600
So Adam points instead,

836
00:36:28,600 --> 00:36:31,600
not to any reciprocal benefit

837
00:36:31,600 --> 00:36:33,600
or mutual exchange,

838
00:36:33,600 --> 00:36:35,600
but to the mere fact

839
00:36:35,600 --> 00:36:37,600
of the agreement itself.

840
00:36:37,600 --> 00:36:38,600
We see here there are really

841
00:36:38,600 --> 00:36:40,600
two different ways in which

842
00:36:40,600 --> 00:36:45,600
actual contracts generate obligations.

843
00:36:45,600 --> 00:36:48,600
One has to do with the act of obligation

844
00:36:48,600 --> 00:36:51,600
of consent as a voluntary act.

845
00:36:51,600 --> 00:36:52,600
And it points,

846
00:36:52,600 --> 00:36:55,600
Adam said this was a Kantian idea,

847
00:36:55,600 --> 00:36:56,600
and I think he's right,

848
00:36:56,600 --> 00:36:59,600
because it points to the ideal of autonomy.

849
00:36:59,600 --> 00:37:01,600
When I make a contract,

850
00:37:01,600 --> 00:37:05,600
the obligation is one that is self-imposed.

851
00:37:05,600 --> 00:37:08,600
And that carries a certain moral weight,

852
00:37:08,600 --> 00:37:12,600
independent of other considerations.

853
00:37:12,600 --> 00:37:15,600
And then there's a second element

854
00:37:15,600 --> 00:37:18,600
of the moral force of contract arguments.

855
00:37:18,600 --> 00:37:20,600
Which has to do with the sense in which

856
00:37:20,600 --> 00:37:22,600
actual contracts are instruments

857
00:37:22,600 --> 00:37:24,600
of mutual benefit.

858
00:37:24,600 --> 00:37:27,600
And this points toward the ideal

859
00:37:27,600 --> 00:37:29,600
of reciprocity.

860
00:37:29,600 --> 00:37:31,600
That obligation can arise,

861
00:37:31,600 --> 00:37:34,600
I can have an obligation to you,

862
00:37:34,600 --> 00:37:42,600
insofar as you do something for me.

863
00:37:42,600 --> 00:37:45,600
Now, we're investigating

864
00:37:45,600 --> 00:37:48,600
the moral force and also the moral limits.

865
00:37:48,600 --> 00:37:52,600
Of actual contracts.

866
00:37:52,600 --> 00:37:56,600
And here I would like to advance an argument

867
00:37:56,600 --> 00:38:01,600
about the moral limits of actual contracts.

868
00:38:01,600 --> 00:38:05,600
Now that we know what moral ingredients

869
00:38:05,600 --> 00:38:07,600
do the work when people come together

870
00:38:07,600 --> 00:38:11,600
and say, I will do this if you do that.

871
00:38:11,600 --> 00:38:14,600
I would like to argue first

872
00:38:14,600 --> 00:38:16,600
that the fact that two people agree

873
00:38:16,600 --> 00:38:17,600
to some exchange,

874
00:38:17,600 --> 00:38:19,600
does not mean that the terms

875
00:38:19,600 --> 00:38:26,600
of their agreement are fair.

876
00:38:26,600 --> 00:38:29,600
When my two sons were young,

877
00:38:29,600 --> 00:38:31,600
they collected baseball cards

878
00:38:31,600 --> 00:38:33,600
and traded them.

879
00:38:33,600 --> 00:38:36,600
And one was, there was a two year age,

880
00:38:36,600 --> 00:38:39,600
there is a two year age difference between them.

881
00:38:39,600 --> 00:38:42,600
And so I had to institute a rule

882
00:38:42,600 --> 00:38:44,600
about the trades,

883
00:38:44,600 --> 00:38:47,600
that no trades are fair

884
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:49,600
and no trades are complete

885
00:38:49,600 --> 00:38:51,600
until I had approved it.

886
00:38:53,600 --> 00:38:56,600
And the reason is obvious.

887
00:38:56,600 --> 00:38:59,600
The older one knew more about the value

888
00:38:59,600 --> 00:39:03,600
of these cards and so would take advantage

889
00:39:03,600 --> 00:39:05,600
of the younger one.

890
00:39:05,600 --> 00:39:07,600
So that's why I had to review it

891
00:39:07,600 --> 00:39:09,600
to make sure that the agreement,

892
00:39:09,600 --> 00:39:11,600
that the agreements were fair.

893
00:39:11,600 --> 00:39:14,600
Now you may say, well this is paternalism.

894
00:39:17,600 --> 00:39:19,600
Of course it was.

895
00:39:19,600 --> 00:39:21,600
That's what paternalism is for.

896
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:23,600
That kind of thing.

897
00:39:25,600 --> 00:39:27,600
So what does this show?

898
00:39:27,600 --> 00:39:29,600
What does the baseball card example show?

899
00:39:29,600 --> 00:39:31,600
The fact of an agreement

900
00:39:31,600 --> 00:39:33,600
is not sufficient to establish

901
00:39:33,600 --> 00:39:35,600
the fairness of the terms.

902
00:39:37,600 --> 00:39:40,600
I read some years ago of a case in Chicago.

903
00:39:40,600 --> 00:39:42,600
There was an elderly widow,

904
00:39:42,600 --> 00:39:44,600
an 84 year old widow,

905
00:39:44,600 --> 00:39:45,600
named Rose,

906
00:39:45,600 --> 00:39:48,600
who had a problem in her apartment

907
00:39:48,600 --> 00:39:50,600
with a leaky toilet.

908
00:39:50,600 --> 00:39:53,600
And she signed a contract

909
00:39:53,600 --> 00:39:56,600
with an unscrupulous contractor

910
00:39:56,600 --> 00:40:01,600
who offered to repair her leaky toilet

911
00:40:01,600 --> 00:40:04,600
in exchange for $50,000.

912
00:40:06,600 --> 00:40:08,600
But she had agreed.

913
00:40:08,600 --> 00:40:10,600
She was of sound mind,

914
00:40:10,600 --> 00:40:12,600
maybe terribly naive and unfamiliar

915
00:40:12,600 --> 00:40:14,600
with the price of plumbing.

916
00:40:15,600 --> 00:40:17,600
She had made this agreement.

917
00:40:17,600 --> 00:40:20,600
Luckily, it was discovered.

918
00:40:20,600 --> 00:40:22,600
She went to the bank

919
00:40:22,600 --> 00:40:25,600
and asked to withdraw $25,000.

920
00:40:25,600 --> 00:40:26,600
And the teller said,

921
00:40:26,600 --> 00:40:28,600
what do you need all of that money for?

922
00:40:28,600 --> 00:40:31,600
And she said, well I have a leaky toilet.

923
00:40:33,600 --> 00:40:35,600
And the teller called authorities

924
00:40:35,600 --> 00:40:38,600
and they discovered this unscrupulous contractor.

925
00:40:38,600 --> 00:40:42,600
Now, I suspect that even the most ardent

926
00:40:42,600 --> 00:40:44,600
contractarians in the room,

927
00:40:44,600 --> 00:40:48,600
will agree that the fact

928
00:40:48,600 --> 00:40:51,600
of this woman's agreement

929
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:54,600
is not a sufficient condition

930
00:40:54,600 --> 00:40:57,600
of the agreement being fair.

931
00:40:57,600 --> 00:41:01,600
Is there anyone who will dispute that?

932
00:41:05,600 --> 00:41:06,600
No one.

933
00:41:06,600 --> 00:41:08,600
Am I missing anyone?

934
00:41:08,600 --> 00:41:10,600
Alex, where are you?

935
00:41:10,600 --> 00:41:11,600
Where are you?

936
00:41:11,600 --> 00:41:12,600
Alex, where are you?

937
00:41:12,600 --> 00:41:13,600
Where are you?

938
00:41:14,600 --> 00:41:19,600
So, maybe there's no dispute then

939
00:41:19,600 --> 00:41:21,600
to my first claim

940
00:41:21,600 --> 00:41:23,600
that an actual agreement

941
00:41:23,600 --> 00:41:25,600
is not necessary to their,

942
00:41:25,600 --> 00:41:27,600
is not a sufficient condition

943
00:41:27,600 --> 00:41:30,600
of their being an obligation.

944
00:41:30,600 --> 00:41:34,600
I want to now make a stronger,

945
00:41:34,600 --> 00:41:36,600
maybe more controversial claim

946
00:41:36,600 --> 00:41:38,600
about the moral limits of actual contracts

947
00:41:38,600 --> 00:41:42,600
that a contractual agreement

948
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:43,600
has to offer.

949
00:41:43,600 --> 00:41:46,600
A contract or an act of consent

950
00:41:46,600 --> 00:41:50,600
is not only not sufficient,

951
00:41:50,600 --> 00:41:53,600
but it's not even a necessary condition

952
00:41:53,600 --> 00:41:56,600
of their being an obligation.

953
00:41:56,600 --> 00:41:58,600
And the idea here is

954
00:41:58,600 --> 00:42:00,600
that if there is reciprocity,

955
00:42:00,600 --> 00:42:02,600
if there is an exchange,

956
00:42:02,600 --> 00:42:05,600
then a receipt of benefits,

957
00:42:05,600 --> 00:42:06,600
there can be an obligation

958
00:42:06,600 --> 00:42:09,600
even without an act of consent.

959
00:42:09,600 --> 00:42:12,600
One great example of this,

960
00:42:12,600 --> 00:42:18,600
involves the 18th century philosopher,

961
00:42:18,600 --> 00:42:19,600
the Scottish moral philosopher,

962
00:42:19,600 --> 00:42:21,600
David Hume.

963
00:42:21,600 --> 00:42:23,600
When he was young,

964
00:42:23,600 --> 00:42:27,600
Hume wrote a book arguing against Locke's idea

965
00:42:27,600 --> 00:42:30,600
of an original social contract.

966
00:42:30,600 --> 00:42:33,600
Hume heaps scorn on this contractarian idea.

967
00:42:33,600 --> 00:42:36,600
He said it was a philosophical fiction,

968
00:42:36,600 --> 00:42:38,600
one of the most mysterious

969
00:42:38,600 --> 00:42:40,600
and incomprehensible operations

970
00:42:40,600 --> 00:42:41,600
that can possibly be

971
00:42:41,600 --> 00:42:43,600
imagined,

972
00:42:43,600 --> 00:42:45,600
this idea of the social contract.

973
00:42:45,600 --> 00:42:47,600
Many years later,

974
00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:49,600
when he was 62 years old,

975
00:42:49,600 --> 00:42:51,600
Hume had an experience

976
00:42:51,600 --> 00:42:52,600
that put to the test

977
00:42:52,600 --> 00:42:53,600
his rejection of consent

978
00:42:53,600 --> 00:42:55,600
as the basis of obligation.

979
00:42:55,600 --> 00:42:58,600
Hume had a house in Edinburgh.

980
00:42:58,600 --> 00:42:59,600
He rented it to his friend,

981
00:42:59,600 --> 00:43:01,600
James Boswell,

982
00:43:01,600 --> 00:43:04,600
who in turn sublet it to a subtenant.

983
00:43:04,600 --> 00:43:05,600
The subtenant decided

984
00:43:05,600 --> 00:43:07,600
that the house needed some repairs

985
00:43:07,600 --> 00:43:09,600
and a paint job.

986
00:43:09,600 --> 00:43:10,600
He hired a contractor

987
00:43:10,600 --> 00:43:12,600
to do the work.

988
00:43:12,600 --> 00:43:13,600
The painter did the work

989
00:43:13,600 --> 00:43:16,600
and sent the bill to Hume.

990
00:43:16,600 --> 00:43:17,600
Hume refused to pay

991
00:43:17,600 --> 00:43:20,600
on the grounds that he hadn't consented.

992
00:43:20,600 --> 00:43:22,600
He hadn't hired the painter.

993
00:43:22,600 --> 00:43:25,600
The case went to court.

994
00:43:25,600 --> 00:43:26,600
The contractor said,

995
00:43:26,600 --> 00:43:27,600
it's true,

996
00:43:27,600 --> 00:43:28,600
Hume didn't agree,

997
00:43:28,600 --> 00:43:30,600
but the house needed a painting

998
00:43:30,600 --> 00:43:32,600
and I gave it a very good one.

999
00:43:34,600 --> 00:43:36,600
Hume thought this was a bad argument.

1000
00:43:36,600 --> 00:43:38,600
The only argument this painter makes

1001
00:43:38,600 --> 00:43:39,600
is that the work was necessary

1002
00:43:39,600 --> 00:43:40,600
to be done,

1003
00:43:40,600 --> 00:43:41,600
but this is no good answer

1004
00:43:41,600 --> 00:43:43,600
because by the same rule,

1005
00:43:43,600 --> 00:43:45,600
this painter may go through

1006
00:43:45,600 --> 00:43:46,600
every house in Edinburgh

1007
00:43:46,600 --> 00:43:48,600
and do what he thinks proper to be done

1008
00:43:48,600 --> 00:43:50,600
without the landlord's consent

1009
00:43:50,600 --> 00:43:52,600
and give the same reason,

1010
00:43:52,600 --> 00:43:54,600
that the work was necessary

1011
00:43:54,600 --> 00:43:57,600
and that the house was the better for it.

1012
00:43:57,600 --> 00:44:01,600
So Hume didn't like the theory

1013
00:44:01,600 --> 00:44:03,600
that there could be obligation

1014
00:44:03,600 --> 00:44:05,600
to repay a benefit without consent,

1015
00:44:07,600 --> 00:44:08,600
but the defense failed,

1016
00:44:08,600 --> 00:44:10,600
and he had to pay.

1017
00:44:10,600 --> 00:44:13,600
Let me give you one other example

1018
00:44:13,600 --> 00:44:14,600
of the distinction

1019
00:44:14,600 --> 00:44:16,600
between the consent-based aspect

1020
00:44:16,600 --> 00:44:18,600
of obligations

1021
00:44:18,600 --> 00:44:20,600
and the benefit-based aspect

1022
00:44:20,600 --> 00:44:22,600
and how they sometimes run together.

1023
00:44:22,600 --> 00:44:27,600
This is based on a personal experience.

1024
00:44:27,600 --> 00:44:28,600
Some years ago,

1025
00:44:28,600 --> 00:44:30,600
I was driving across the country

1026
00:44:30,600 --> 00:44:31,600
with some friends,

1027
00:44:31,600 --> 00:44:33,600
and we found ourselves

1028
00:44:33,600 --> 00:44:34,600
in the middle of nowhere

1029
00:44:34,600 --> 00:44:36,600
in Hammond, Indiana.

1030
00:44:36,600 --> 00:44:38,600
We stopped at a restaurant

1031
00:44:38,600 --> 00:44:40,600
and got out of the car,

1032
00:44:40,600 --> 00:44:41,600
and when we came back,

1033
00:44:41,600 --> 00:44:44,600
our car wouldn't start.

1034
00:44:44,600 --> 00:44:47,600
None of us knew much about cars.

1035
00:44:47,600 --> 00:44:49,600
We didn't really know what to do

1036
00:44:49,600 --> 00:44:51,600
until we noticed that

1037
00:44:51,600 --> 00:44:54,600
in the parking lot driving up next to us

1038
00:44:54,600 --> 00:44:56,600
was a van,

1039
00:44:56,600 --> 00:44:57,600
and on the side it said,

1040
00:44:57,600 --> 00:45:01,600
Sam's Mobile Repair Van.

1041
00:45:01,600 --> 00:45:04,600
And out of the van came a man,

1042
00:45:04,600 --> 00:45:06,600
presumably Sam,

1043
00:45:06,600 --> 00:45:08,600
and he came up to us

1044
00:45:08,600 --> 00:45:09,600
and he said,

1045
00:45:09,600 --> 00:45:10,600
Can I help you?

1046
00:45:10,600 --> 00:45:11,600
Here's how I work.

1047
00:45:11,600 --> 00:45:14,600
I work by the hour for $50 an hour.

1048
00:45:14,600 --> 00:45:16,600
If I fix your car in five minutes,

1049
00:45:16,600 --> 00:45:18,600
you owe me the $50,

1050
00:45:18,600 --> 00:45:20,600
and if I work on your car for an hour

1051
00:45:20,600 --> 00:45:21,600
and can't fix it,

1052
00:45:21,600 --> 00:45:24,600
you'll still owe me the $50.

1053
00:45:24,600 --> 00:45:25,600
So I said,

1054
00:45:25,600 --> 00:45:26,600
Well, what is the likelihood

1055
00:45:26,600 --> 00:45:28,600
that you'll be able to fix the car in?

1056
00:45:28,600 --> 00:45:30,600
He didn't answer,

1057
00:45:30,600 --> 00:45:33,600
but he did start looking under the,

1058
00:45:33,600 --> 00:45:36,600
poking around under the steering column.

1059
00:45:36,600 --> 00:45:37,600
Short time passed.

1060
00:45:37,600 --> 00:45:40,600
He emerged from under the steering column and said,

1061
00:45:40,600 --> 00:45:42,600
There's nothing wrong with the ignition system,

1062
00:45:42,600 --> 00:45:44,600
but you still have 45 minutes left.

1063
00:45:44,600 --> 00:45:46,600
Should I look under the hood?

1064
00:45:48,600 --> 00:45:49,600
I said,

1065
00:45:49,600 --> 00:45:50,600
Wait a minute.

1066
00:45:50,600 --> 00:45:52,600
I haven't hired you.

1067
00:45:52,600 --> 00:45:54,600
We haven't made any agreement.

1068
00:45:54,600 --> 00:45:57,600
And then he became very angry,

1069
00:45:57,600 --> 00:45:58,600
and he said,

1070
00:45:58,600 --> 00:46:00,100
Do you mean to say that

1071
00:46:00,100 --> 00:46:01,600
if I had fixed your car

1072
00:46:01,600 --> 00:46:03,600
while I was working under the steering column

1073
00:46:03,600 --> 00:46:06,600
that you wouldn't have paid me?

1074
00:46:06,600 --> 00:46:08,600
And I said,

1075
00:46:08,600 --> 00:46:10,600
That's a different question.

1076
00:46:19,600 --> 00:46:21,600
I didn't go into the distinction

1077
00:46:21,600 --> 00:46:24,600
between consent-based and benefit-based obligations.

1078
00:46:29,600 --> 00:46:31,600
But I think he had the intuition

1079
00:46:31,600 --> 00:46:32,600
that if he had fixed

1080
00:46:32,600 --> 00:46:33,600
it while he was poking around,

1081
00:46:33,600 --> 00:46:35,600
that I would have owed him the $50,

1082
00:46:35,600 --> 00:46:38,600
I shared that intuition.

1083
00:46:38,600 --> 00:46:39,600
I would have.

1084
00:46:39,600 --> 00:46:41,600
But he inferred from that,

1085
00:46:41,600 --> 00:46:44,600
this was the fallacy and the reasoning

1086
00:46:44,600 --> 00:46:46,600
that I think lay behind his anger,

1087
00:46:46,600 --> 00:46:50,600
he inferred from that fact that therefore,

1088
00:46:50,600 --> 00:46:52,600
implicitly,

1089
00:46:52,600 --> 00:46:54,600
we had an agreement.

1090
00:46:56,600 --> 00:46:58,600
But that, it seems to me, is a mistake.

1091
00:46:58,600 --> 00:47:00,600
It's a mistake that fails to recognize the distinction

1092
00:47:00,600 --> 00:47:04,600
between these two different aspects of contract arguments.

1093
00:47:04,600 --> 00:47:07,600
Yes, I agree, I would have owed him $50

1094
00:47:07,600 --> 00:47:09,600
if he had repaired my car during that time.

1095
00:47:09,600 --> 00:47:13,600
Not because we had made any agreement,

1096
00:47:13,600 --> 00:47:15,600
we hadn't.

1097
00:47:15,600 --> 00:47:17,600
But simply because if he had fixed my car,

1098
00:47:17,600 --> 00:47:19,600
he would have conferred on me a benefit

1099
00:47:19,600 --> 00:47:21,600
for which I would have owed him

1100
00:47:21,600 --> 00:47:25,600
in the name of reciprocity and fairness.

1101
00:47:25,600 --> 00:47:27,600
So, here's another example

1102
00:47:27,600 --> 00:47:29,600
of the distinction between these

1103
00:47:29,600 --> 00:47:31,600
two different kinds of arguments,

1104
00:47:31,600 --> 00:47:33,600
these two different aspects

1105
00:47:33,600 --> 00:47:36,600
of the morality of contract.

1106
00:47:36,600 --> 00:47:38,600
Now, I want to hear how many think

1107
00:47:38,600 --> 00:47:40,600
I was in the right in that case.

1108
00:47:42,600 --> 00:47:44,600
That's reassuring.

1109
00:47:44,600 --> 00:47:47,600
Is there anyone who thinks I was in the wrong?

1110
00:47:50,600 --> 00:47:52,600
Anyone?

1111
00:47:54,600 --> 00:47:56,600
You do?

1112
00:47:56,600 --> 00:47:58,600
Why? Go ahead.

1113
00:47:58,600 --> 00:48:00,600
Isn't the problem with this is that

1114
00:48:00,600 --> 00:48:02,600
any benefit is inherently subjectively defined?

1115
00:48:02,600 --> 00:48:04,600
I mean, what if you wanted your car broken

1116
00:48:04,600 --> 00:48:06,600
and he had fixed it?

1117
00:48:06,600 --> 00:48:07,600
I mean...

1118
00:48:07,600 --> 00:48:09,600
No, I didn't want it broken.

1119
00:48:09,600 --> 00:48:11,600
Yeah, in this case.

1120
00:48:11,600 --> 00:48:13,600
But who would? Who would?

1121
00:48:13,600 --> 00:48:15,600
I don't know, someone.

1122
00:48:15,600 --> 00:48:17,600
I mean, what if Hume, you know,

1123
00:48:17,600 --> 00:48:20,600
what if the painter had painted his house blue

1124
00:48:20,600 --> 00:48:22,600
but he hated the color blue?

1125
00:48:22,600 --> 00:48:26,600
I mean, you have to sort of define

1126
00:48:26,600 --> 00:48:29,600
what your benefit is before the person does it.

1127
00:48:29,600 --> 00:48:31,600
Well, all right.

1128
00:48:31,600 --> 00:48:34,600
So, what would you conclude from that, though,

1129
00:48:34,600 --> 00:48:36,600
for the larger issue here?

1130
00:48:36,600 --> 00:48:38,600
Would you conclude that, therefore, consent

1131
00:48:38,600 --> 00:48:41,600
is a necessary condition of there being an obligation?

1132
00:48:41,600 --> 00:48:42,600
Absolutely.

1133
00:48:42,600 --> 00:48:44,600
You would. What's your name?

1134
00:48:44,600 --> 00:48:46,600
Nate.

1135
00:48:46,600 --> 00:48:49,600
Because otherwise, how can we know, Nate says,

1136
00:48:49,600 --> 00:48:52,600
whether there has been an exchange

1137
00:48:52,600 --> 00:48:55,600
of equivalent or fair benefits?

1138
00:48:55,600 --> 00:48:57,600
Unless we have the subjective valuation,

1139
00:48:57,600 --> 00:48:59,600
which may vary one person to the next

1140
00:48:59,600 --> 00:49:01,600
of the situation.

1141
00:49:01,600 --> 00:49:03,600
All right, that's a fair challenge.

1142
00:49:03,600 --> 00:49:06,600
Let me put to you one other example

1143
00:49:06,600 --> 00:49:10,600
in order to test the relation between these two aspects

1144
00:49:10,600 --> 00:49:12,600
of the morality of contract.

1145
00:49:12,600 --> 00:49:14,600
Suppose I get married.

1146
00:49:14,600 --> 00:49:17,600
And suppose I discover

1147
00:49:17,600 --> 00:49:21,600
that after 20 years of faithfulness on my part,

1148
00:49:21,600 --> 00:49:24,600
every year on our trip across the country,

1149
00:49:24,600 --> 00:49:27,600
my wife has been seeing another man.

1150
00:49:27,600 --> 00:49:30,600
A man with a van on the Indiana toll road.

1151
00:49:30,600 --> 00:49:33,600
This part is completely made up, by the way.

1152
00:49:36,600 --> 00:49:41,600
Wouldn't I have two different reasons for moral outrage?

1153
00:49:41,600 --> 00:49:44,600
One reason could be, we had an agreement.

1154
00:49:44,600 --> 00:49:47,100
She broke her promise, referring

1155
00:49:47,100 --> 00:49:49,600
to the fact of her consent.

1156
00:49:49,600 --> 00:49:52,100
But I would also have

1157
00:49:52,100 --> 00:49:54,600
a second ground for moral outrage,

1158
00:49:54,600 --> 00:49:57,600
having nothing to do with the contract as such.

1159
00:49:57,600 --> 00:49:59,600
But I've been so faithful for my part.

1160
00:49:59,600 --> 00:50:02,600
Surely I deserve better than this.

1161
00:50:02,600 --> 00:50:04,600
Is this what I'm due in return?

1162
00:50:04,600 --> 00:50:05,600
And so on.

1163
00:50:05,600 --> 00:50:08,600
So that would point to the element of reciprocity.

1164
00:50:08,600 --> 00:50:13,600
Each reason has an independent moral force.

1165
00:50:13,600 --> 00:50:15,600
That's the general point.

1166
00:50:15,600 --> 00:50:17,100
And you can see this

1167
00:50:17,100 --> 00:50:18,600
if you imagine a slight variation

1168
00:50:18,600 --> 00:50:20,600
on the marriage case.

1169
00:50:20,600 --> 00:50:23,600
Suppose we hadn't been married for 20 years.

1170
00:50:23,600 --> 00:50:26,600
Suppose we were just married,

1171
00:50:26,600 --> 00:50:29,600
and that the betrayal looked like a lie.

1172
00:50:29,600 --> 00:50:32,600
And that it occurred on the way to our honeymoon

1173
00:50:32,600 --> 00:50:34,600
in Hammond, Indiana.

1174
00:50:34,600 --> 00:50:37,600
After the contract has been made,

1175
00:50:37,600 --> 00:50:39,600
but before there is any

1176
00:50:39,600 --> 00:50:41,600
history of performance on my part.

1177
00:50:44,600 --> 00:50:47,600
Performance of the contract, I mean.

1178
00:50:52,600 --> 00:50:53,600
I would still...

1179
00:50:53,600 --> 00:50:56,600
Oh, come on, come on.

1180
00:50:56,600 --> 00:50:59,600
I would still, with Judy,

1181
00:50:59,600 --> 00:51:01,600
I would be able to say,

1182
00:51:01,600 --> 00:51:02,600
but you promised.

1183
00:51:02,600 --> 00:51:03,600
You promised.

1184
00:51:03,600 --> 00:51:08,600
That would isolate the pure element of consent, right?

1185
00:51:08,600 --> 00:51:11,600
Where there were no benefit.

1186
00:51:11,600 --> 00:51:12,600
Never mind.

1187
00:51:12,600 --> 00:51:13,600
You get the idea.

1188
00:51:19,600 --> 00:51:21,600
Here's the main idea.

1189
00:51:22,600 --> 00:51:24,600
Actual contracts

1190
00:51:26,600 --> 00:51:28,600
have their moral force in virtue,

1191
00:51:29,600 --> 00:51:32,600
of two distinguishable ideals.

1192
00:51:32,600 --> 00:51:35,600
Autonomy and reciprocity.

1193
00:51:35,600 --> 00:51:38,600
But in real life,

1194
00:51:38,600 --> 00:51:41,600
every actual contract

1195
00:51:41,600 --> 00:51:44,600
may fall short,

1196
00:51:44,600 --> 00:51:46,600
may fail to realize

1197
00:51:46,600 --> 00:51:50,600
the ideals that give contracts their moral force

1198
00:51:50,600 --> 00:51:52,600
in the first place.

1199
00:51:54,600 --> 00:51:56,600
The ideal of autonomy may not be realized

1200
00:51:56,600 --> 00:51:58,600
because there may be a difference in the bargaining.

1201
00:51:58,600 --> 00:52:01,600
There may be a difference in the bargaining power

1202
00:52:01,600 --> 00:52:03,600
of the parties.

1203
00:52:06,600 --> 00:52:09,600
The ideal of reciprocity may not be realized

1204
00:52:09,600 --> 00:52:12,600
because there may be a difference of knowledge

1205
00:52:12,600 --> 00:52:14,600
between the parties.

1206
00:52:14,600 --> 00:52:17,600
And so they may misidentify

1207
00:52:17,600 --> 00:52:22,600
what really counts as having equivalent value.

1208
00:52:23,600 --> 00:52:25,600
Now,

1209
00:52:25,600 --> 00:52:27,600
suppose you were to imagine

1210
00:52:27,600 --> 00:52:29,600
a contract

1211
00:52:29,600 --> 00:52:32,600
where the ideals of autonomy

1212
00:52:32,600 --> 00:52:34,600
and of reciprocity

1213
00:52:34,600 --> 00:52:37,600
were not subject to contingency,

1214
00:52:37,600 --> 00:52:39,600
but were guaranteed to be realized.

1215
00:52:39,600 --> 00:52:42,600
What kind of contract would that have to be?

1216
00:52:43,600 --> 00:52:45,600
Imagine a contract among

1217
00:52:45,600 --> 00:52:48,600
parties who were equal in power and knowledge

1218
00:52:48,600 --> 00:52:50,600
rather than unequal,

1219
00:52:50,600 --> 00:52:52,600
who were identically situated

1220
00:52:52,600 --> 00:52:54,600
rather than differently situated.

1221
00:52:54,600 --> 00:52:56,600
That is the idea

1222
00:52:56,600 --> 00:53:00,600
behind Rawls's claim

1223
00:53:00,600 --> 00:53:03,600
that the way to think about justice

1224
00:53:03,600 --> 00:53:06,600
is from the standpoint of a hypothetical contract

1225
00:53:06,600 --> 00:53:08,600
behind a veil of ignorance

1226
00:53:08,600 --> 00:53:11,600
that creates a condition of equality

1227
00:53:11,600 --> 00:53:14,600
by ruling out

1228
00:53:14,600 --> 00:53:17,600
or enabling us to forget for the moment

1229
00:53:17,600 --> 00:53:20,600
the differences in power and knowledge

1230
00:53:20,600 --> 00:53:24,600
that could, even in principle,

1231
00:53:24,600 --> 00:53:27,600
lead to unfair results.

1232
00:53:27,600 --> 00:53:29,600
This is why

1233
00:53:29,600 --> 00:53:31,600
for Kant and for Rawls,

1234
00:53:31,600 --> 00:53:34,600
a hypothetical contract among equals

1235
00:53:34,600 --> 00:53:36,600
is the only way

1236
00:53:36,600 --> 00:53:39,600
to think about principles of justice.

1237
00:53:39,600 --> 00:53:41,600
What will those principles be?

1238
00:53:41,600 --> 00:53:43,600
That's the question we'll turn to next time.

